over the course of eight years President  Obama was shadowed by former White House  photographer Pete Souza his photos  captured nearly every moment during the  44th presidents two terms Souza  sometimes took 2000 photos a day his new  book Obama an intimate portrait features  more than 300 photos he takes readers  behind the scenes with President Obama  Pete Souza good morning thank you for  having me it's a beautiful book it's  really well done and I love the foreword  by the former president who says I  probably spent more time with Pete Souza  than anybody other than my family in the  eight years in the White House you were  a constant presence and chronicler of  history I was it was a great privilege  to do this job I feel fortunate that I  had the opportunity this is as good a  picture I've ever seen I think  photograph but do you have one that you  think defies or sort of portrays the  president more than any other single  photograph here that captures him you  know I saw him in so many walks of life  that it's hard to choose any one  certainly some of the one of him  interacting with kids or with his family  but also the more weighty pictures of  him in the Situation Room  during the bin Laden raid so there's I  think the pictures together tell you the  most about him in any one picture well  you certainly got to see the variety of  him you got to see a sense of humor I  love the one page 102 where his foot is  on the scale of someone who works with  him and everybody he clearly was playing  a joke on the guy and everybody was in  on the joke  yeah Marvin Nicholson was he was  checking his way because he had been  trying to try to wait and he kept  sliding the the scale to the right and  I'm he notes to him the president's got  his toe on the scale that was funny  you've got hecklers backstage I thought  that was interesting that you're the  president is speaking and he's heckled  but he calls the hecklers backstage so  if you guys must have freaked about that  he it was an immigration policy speech  and there were these two guys that were  heckling him during his speech and he  said let me finish my speech and then  after  where do you come backstage and I'll  have a private conversation view and  that's exactly what he did  and you see The Secret Service agent  there were there ever moments and I know  the president praised you for being  almost invisible he didn't know  sometimes you're in there were there  ever moments where you thought this is  too personal I I shouldn't be here or  there shouldn't be a photograph of this  moment not when it came to moments of  history with moments with the family yes  I would try to give him space moments of  history I felt you know I am the guy  that needs to be there for everything  that happens so in those cases know Jim  Baker has said about being Secretary of  State you really have to have the  complete trust of the president this is  the same thing prevent photography yes  absolutely have to have that  relationship salutely and I think well I  think you earn it over time I mean I was  lucky in that I had known him for four  years before he became president had  already established the professional  relationship with him so coming in I  already knew him that helped but then  over time you just have to earn that  trust day in and day out one of the most  famous photos that I know was displayed  in the White House for a long time was  of a young boy touching President  Obama's hair my favorite photo yes that  one that photo became so iconic because  why oh I think for two reasons one the  little boy's name is Jacob Philadelphia  he's touching the the man who's the  president and he looks like him but it  also tells you something about President  Obama where he would be willing to bend  over like that and let a four-year-old  just touches touch his head  you said of that picture say your hair  is just like your hair is just like mine  that's why I think that photo is so  beautiful and you said of all the  pictures that stayed up the longest  normally you take them down and you  recycle them but that one kept coming  back notice people wanted that to see  then I just we took it down at one point  and I had  individual staff members come into my  office asking me to put it back up I  love all the pictures of the president  with it with it with children him with a  super soaker gun we have a picture  that's a very private moment he's  clearly with the family I think it was  in camp days at Camp David running  around the pool squirting Sasha yeah I  thought that would like to be there in  the Situation Room when they're watching  the Obama Ben love scene in London yeah  the the thing that I like to tell people  about that photograph is you have the  most powerful people in the federal  government watching as this is unfolding  and really there's nothing they can do  they've already made their decision and  now it's up to those guys on the ground  and I think that's what leads to the  anxiety and all those phases but I love  the back story you told about that Pete  they just said we need you to be here at  such-and-such time you didn't know what  was going to happen he said it's a very  small room and you just you said I have  to get in a corner and just stay and you  were trying to figure out which corner  should I be in who tells you that the  chief of staff no I had about a week's  notice that something was gonna be  unfolding I was actually someone in  John's brennan's office that alerted me  so I knew it was going to be on that  Sunday and it was actually you know we  were there all day  yeah it wasn't just that one 40-minute  meeting in that little room but there  were meetings throughout the day what's  your takeaway of the man who knew him  before he was president while he was  president and then after what's your  takeaway of Barack Obama right my  takeaway is you know I met him his first  day in the Senate in 2005 that was the  first